# CrudeCode SPEE Bake-Off SmartCast v1.4.1

Deterministic empirical decline-curve forecasting and validation tooling for the SPEE Software Symposium bake-off.

## v1.5 selective cohort candidate

`selective_cohort_v1` is the only new forecasting profile. It applies the
unchanged conservative play cohort to the oil phase only when the target is
oil-primary and its supplied play is DJ, Eagle Ford, or Delaware. Exact SciPy
is returned everywhere else. The route uses no provenance or producing-days
fields.

The policy was selected on spent development evidence and must pass one fixed
`confirm3` run before production use:

```powershell
python scripts/run_v15_selective_confirmation.py `
  --board "board/private-v15" `
  --legacy-src "C:\path\to\forecast-benchmark-main\src" `
  --smartcast-src ".\src" `
  --boot 30000 `
  --out "board/results/v15-selective-confirm3" `
  --report-only
```

Until that gate passes, Strict Auto remains `--profile scipy_control`.

## v1.4.1 provenance hardening

The real-board extractor now captures warehouse reporting provenance without
exposing it to the forecasting model. It supports whole-well `mixed`,
`reported_holdout`, `reported_full`, and threshold gates; it never deletes
individual DCA rows. A new audit quantifies reporting methods and producing-day
patterns by play/role, and a fixed confirmation runner tests `gated_cohort_v1`
only on a board whose scored holdout is entirely `REPORTED`.

See `docs/V1_4_1_PROVENANCE_HARDENING.md`. Production remains
`--profile scipy_control` until a provenance-corrected confirmation passes.


## v1.4 dispersion attack

The exact SciPy control remains the default. v1.4 adds one targeted challenger,
`gated_cohort_v1`, built only from the replicated play-specific cohort signal.
It caps cohort authority, requires age-specific peer support, rejects dispersed
or dissimilar cohorts, suppresses endpoint anomalies, bounds divergence from
SciPy and blends in log-rate space. It does not add new curve families or a
black-box selector.

Use the spent pools only for tail diagnosis:

```powershell
python scripts/run_v14_tail_diagnostics.py `
  --board "board/private-v131" `
  --boot 10000 `
  --out "board/results/v14-tail"
```

A new `confirm3` role and fixed one-look runner are available for the single
pre-registered v1.4 confirmation. See
`docs/V1_4_DISPERSION_ATTACK.md` for the exact extraction command and promotion
gates. Production remains `--profile scipy_control` until that run passes.

## Current production position

The 299-well real development board established the original SciPy bounded-Arps implementation as the current control:

| Arm | Major-phase SPEE | Median log error | Spread |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Original SciPy Arps | **0.1209** | -0.0016 | 0.3595 |
| Fast linearized Arps | 0.2098 | -0.0213 | 0.5870 |
| SmartCast v1.1/v1.2 stack | 0.1643 | -0.0327 | 0.4273 |

SmartCast was significantly worse than the control on that development board. v1.3 therefore does **not** declare the richer stack the production winner. The safe default is `scipy_control`, an exact packaged copy of the original control. Experimental layers must earn promotion on a development tune/confirmation split.

## What v1.3 fixes

- Restores the actual original SciPy bounded-Arps implementation as the safety anchor.
- Keeps Claude's valid NaN-repair fix in terminal-decline post-processing.
- Adds SciPy as a required runtime dependency.
- Fixes a major metadata defect: target dev/eval wells now receive their play/formation metadata instead of silently using the mixed `__global__` cohort.
- Makes the terminal-decline ablation switch real at every forecast path.
- Adds support-aware play cohorts and an option to prohibit global fallback.
- Adds deterministic fit and anchor caches across profile comparisons.
- Adds a fast control path that avoids fitting unused candidate models.
- Replaces broad tuning with a small pre-registered profile board and a deterministic tune/confirmation protocol.
- Makes the competition runners default to the proven control rather than an unvalidated full stack.

See `docs/V1_3_ACCURACY_HARDENING.md` for the audit and decision logic.

